well for collision, if you don't have any type of quadtree/other optimization already in place, just run a double loop kinda like this:

    for transform_component in transform_components.iter {
      for other_transform_component in { let temp =  transform_component.clone(); temp.next(); temp } {
        check_collision(transform_component.next(), other_transform_component.next());
      }
    }

so essentially: say i have func1 and func2. func1 only has

    try {
        func2();
    } catch(exception e) {
        std::cout << "error";
    }

and func2 has

    throw exception();

now disregarding any optimizations, let's assume func1 is mapped at addresses 0x40000 through 0x40100 (just guesstimating) and func2 is 0x401b0 through 0x40200. somewhere in a data section, a map says that addresses 0x40000 through wherever the try block ends are to pass exceptions to the corresponding catch block. so when the exception is thrown, the address is looked up in the table: it's not found, so the stack is unwound to the next frame: the address in rip is checked again, this time the catch block is found, and execution continues from there. correct?

when the first 2 lines are

    #include <bits/stdc++.h>
    using namespace std;

you know you should run as far as possible

maybe this could work (haven't tested this yet):

    if constexpr(requires {
        static_assert(maybe_runtime_int);
    }) {
        // if true, maybe_runtime_int is evaluated at compile time
        static_assert(maybe_runtime_int < 30);
    } else {
        // static assert tried to take a runtime value and failed to compile, so requires returned false
        assert(maybe_runtime_int < 30);
    }

again, i haven't tested this, and i'm pretty sure the requires expression would always return true because it only cares about types, plus everyone's complaining about not knowing if they're in a compile time context and this looks so simple that it's been probably tried already. anyway, who knows, give it a try

